{
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "scope": "user",
  "platforms": [
    "linux"
  ],
  "name": "Notifications",
  "description": "A local log of this computer's desktop notifications, so JARVIS can answer 'what did I miss?': Discord, Telegram, Steam, mail, a browser's web notifications (WhatsApp Web, Gmail...) and a phone's apps through KDE Connect, grouped by app and sender, plus a search over the last days. It reads no account and automates nothing: it only sees what apps already showed as notifications, and only while the user is logged in. A small collector (a systemd user service, no root) writes the log; one-time codes are blanked and JARVIS's own notifications dropped before anything is stored, the log is owner-only and kept 7 days by default, and nothing leaves the computer.",
  "summary": "What did I miss? A local, owner-only log of this computer's notifications (Discord, Telegram, WhatsApp Web, Steam, mail, phone apps through KDE Connect), grouped by app and sender, and searchable. One-time codes blanked; no account touched; nothing leaves the computer. Python standard library and systemd's busctl.",
  "keywords": [
    "notifications",
    "what did i miss",
    "missed messages",
    "unread",
    "catch up",
    "discord messages",
    "telegram messages",
    "whatsapp messages",
    "phone notifications",
    "kde connect",
    "desktop notifications",
    "notification history",
    "who messaged me",
    "messages while away",
    "search notifications"
  ],
  "homepage": "https://github.com/JarvisOSLinux/mcp-registry",
  "transports": [
    {
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "python3",
      "args": [
        "server.py"
      ],
      "platforms": [
        "linux"
      ],
      "description": "Notifications MCP Server (Linux, freedesktop notifications on the session bus)"
    }
  ],
  "source": {
    "type": "git",
    "url": "https://github.com/JarvisOSLinux/mcp-registry.git",
    "rev": "4153c56c591cd4a4ef2e90cdd8120fc35539794a",
    "path": "servers/notifications"
  },
  "setupScript": "setup.sh",
  "tools": [
    {
      "name": "what_did_i_miss",
      "description": "Notifications since the user last asked (or the last N hours), grouped by app and sender, with each sender's latest messages",
      "threat_level": "elevated"
    },
    {
      "name": "search_notifications",
      "description": "Search the kept notifications for words in the sender, text or app",
      "threat_level": "elevated"
    },
    {
      "name": "notification_apps",
      "description": "Which apps sent notifications and how many, retention, what is never logged, and the collector's status; no message text",
      "threat_level": "safe"
    },
    {
      "name": "forget_notifications",
      "description": "Delete notifications from JARVIS's log, from named apps or all of them",
      "threat_level": "elevated"
    }
  ],
  "configurableProperties": [
    {
      "key": "NOTIFICATIONS_EXCLUDE",
      "label": "Never log these apps",
      "description": "Optional. App names to leave out, separated by commas (e.g. 'KeePassXC, Signal'). Their notifications are never stored, and ones already stored are deleted. JARVIS's own notifications are always left out.",
      "sensitive": false,
      "required": false
    },
    {
      "key": "NOTIFICATIONS_KEEP_DAYS",
      "label": "Keep for (days)",
      "description": "Optional. How many days notifications are kept, 1-90. Default 7.",
      "sensitive": false,
      "required": false,
      "default": "7"
    }
  ],
  "trust": {
    "reviewReferences": [
      "https://specifications.freedesktop.org/notification-spec/latest/",
      "https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/busctl.html",
      "https://github.com/JarvisOSLinux/mcp-registry/tree/main/servers/notifications"
    ],
    "checks": [
      "license: server code is first-party, under this repository's licence",
      "source: maintained in-tree at servers/notifications, pinned by commit",
      "dependencies: none beyond the Python standard library (sqlite3, json, subprocess) and systemd's busctl",
      "network: none \u2014 no tool and no part of the collector opens a network connection",
      "dbus: the collector only monitors org.freedesktop.Notifications.Notify calls on the user's own session bus (busctl --user monitor); it never sends, answers, closes or changes a notification",
      "persistence: setup.sh installs and starts the systemd user service jarvis-notifications.service (no root, NoNewPrivileges); it is skipped once the server's folder is removed",
      "filesystem: an owner-only SQLite log and settings file under $XDG_STATE_HOME/jarvis-notifications (0700 folder, 0600 files)"
    ],
    "notes": "First-party server maintained in this repository. Linux keeps no notification log of its own (the freedesktop spec has none, and Plasma holds its history in memory only), so a collector has to listen while the user is logged in; it is a systemd user service so it also runs when JARVIS doesn't. It sees exactly what apps chose to show: muted chats, and a chat app's open conversation, don't notify. Phone notifications arrive through KDE Connect and are grouped under the phone app.",
    "securityNotes": "what_did_i_miss and search_notifications are elevated: notifications carry private messages, and their text is written by other people (a prompt-injection surface, marked as data in every result). forget_notifications is elevated: it deletes from JARVIS's own log, never from the apps. notification_apps is safe: app names and counts only.\n\nBefore anything is stored, one-time codes (digit runs in a notification that talks about codes, in several languages) are replaced with [code hidden], JARVIS's own notifications (its sign-in codes and approval prompts) are dropped, and apps in NOTIFICATIONS_EXCLUDE are dropped; adding an app there also deletes what was stored from it. The log is owner-only and pruned after NOTIFICATIONS_KEEP_DAYS (7 by default). With a hosted model, what these tools return goes to that provider, like any tool result: with a local model it never leaves the computer."
  },
